{
  "case_id": "2008-07-588",
  "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Environmental Protection Agency",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For more than 30 years, EPA has construed the Clean Water Act to permit it to consider the relationship between costs and benefits in setting limits on water intake. The court of appeals' unprecedented limitation of that discretion is wrong as a matter of basic Chevron interpretive principles for at least three reasons."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear argument first today in Case 07-588, Entergy Corporation v. Riverkeeper Incorporated, and the consolidated cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: The Second Circuit recognized that latter kind of taking account of costs disproportionate, more than the industry would bear, and they also recognized a cost comparison. If you have a cheaper method that is almost as good, you can use that and you don't have to use the one that will capture the extra fish. So everybody agrees that there is some consideration of cost. The question is how much, and the concern is, as Justice Souter says, that you are comparing things that aren't comparable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAHONEY: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: I would like to start with just setting the stage here. For almost 30 years now, the Executive Branch has had an executive order through all administrations that requires a cost-benefit analysis to be done whenever regulations are adopted. And that's because the Executive Branch considers that to be just an essential component of reasoned decisionmaking."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Okay, fine. Then what are you arguing? Are you arguing that you should take costs into account?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAHONEY",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are not arguing that you have to do monetized cost-benefit analysis, nor did the agency say that it was basing its rule on --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I am not clear. I -- I did not understand that for 30 years the only test has been a grossly disproportionate test."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAHONEY",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Wholly disproportionate, not grossly disproportionate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. LAZARUS",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In section 316(b), Congress did not authorize EPA to decide that the benefits of minimizing adverse environmental impact did not justify the cost of available technology."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Except -- except, you say, when it's grossly disproportionate?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice ALITO",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I don't want to interrupt you in your enumeration of three reasons, but I just don't see how you get cost into the concept of availability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. LAZARUS",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Availability includes economic availability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. LAZARUS",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0001-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But for best available there is no cost-benefit analysis. It's only for best practical --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lazarus",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LAZARUS: Well, I think it's not ambiguous language because you look at the statutory language, it says -- right? That's in section 316(b) -- EPA is required -- right? Instructed, required, that location, design, construction, capacity of cooling water intake structures reflect the best technology available for minimizing adverse environmental impact. And what we argue, and I think this is quite compelling, Your Honor, is that with that language Congress itself struck the cost-benefit analysis. Congress said for costs, EPA has to ensure the technology is available. Either on industry wide, and could take site into account site-specific factors. For benefits, Congress said EPA has to ensure that the environmental benefits -- the adverse environmental impact -- be minimized to the extent it can be done with available technology."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: But if the only question, when you say they can consider costs, are you saying they are simply asking whether it is economically possible for the industry to afford this, regardless of whether it's any good or not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: And did that section 307 not allow cost-benefit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lazarus",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0003-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "in 1972, Your Honor, there was only one provision of the Clean Water Act in which Congress delegated any authority to EPA to compare costs and benefits; and that was under 301(b)(2)"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: You would have to agree, wouldn't you, that the panel's decision in this case overruled the prior panel's decision in judge Katzman's decision in Riverkeeper 1?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Environmental Protection Agency",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If I could start where I left off, the fact that Riverkeeper 1 in their view is wrong, I think does confirm what anyone would conclude after reading the two opinions, which is that they are not consistent. At a minimum that helps to demonstrate ambiguity."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Environmental Protection Agency",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0003-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What EPA did here was not among the most robust forms of cost-benefit analysis. In other words we don't think the Court necessarily needs to expand -- to opine on the outer limit here. EPA did two things. First, as you mentioned, it first created national performance standards; second it did a variance when an facility's costs are significantly greater than the benefits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Environmental Protection Agency",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ade17efb97f0-0003-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 63,
        "start": 63
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One of the main points here is that I really don't think Respondents have any logic to their position, because when we are talking about the text they say costs have to be considered against affordability and not against benefits. But when we are talking about absurd results, they say, oh sure, you can consider costs against benefits, when it would otherwise be absurd. But under Chevron it's the agency's gap-filling discretion to decide when where to draw that line, and nothing draw a de minimis line in the statute any more than the line EPA has drawn."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-07-588",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "ade17efb97f08f985a290cbdf74a8def3e3af0c314375330f664e90e79f7d002",
